1
00:02:39,492 --> 00:02:41,403
<i>Here Friday morning.</i>

3
00:02:45,031 --> 00:02:49,604
<i>The Financial Times 100 Index up 11 points,
just above the key 3,800 level.</i>

4
00:02:49,869 --> 00:02:54,545
<i>In the rest of Europe, Frankfurt's DAX
gaining nine points to 2,532.</i>

5
00:02:54,707 --> 00:02:58,711
<i>In Paris, the CAC 40 losing seven,
the weaker franc weighing on the market.</i>

6
00:03:13,593 --> 00:03:15,265
Thank you, Ilsa.

7
00:03:16,396 --> 00:03:18,034
Have a nice day.

8
00:03:30,510 --> 00:03:32,614
<i>...they are on time this morning.</i>

9
00:03:33,913 --> 00:03:35,585
<i>San Francisco's freeways are...</i>

10
00:03:51,631 --> 00:03:53,298
Morning, sir.

11
00:03:53,333 --> 00:03:54,999
<i>Tell me
what we're supposed to do.</i>

12
00:03:55,034 --> 00:04:00,573
<i>You may be perched on majority shares,
but we all get fucked if the actuals crash.</i>

13
00:04:00,608 --> 00:04:02,006
<i>That old man is gonna sink us.</i>

14
00:04:02,041 --> 00:04:06,956
As soon as I have Baer/Grant's
P&L report in hand, I will call you.

15
00:04:07,614 --> 00:04:10,982
<i>- Is that a promise?
- I'm sorry, I'm not aware of that term.</i>

16
00:04:11,017 --> 00:04:14,921
<i>Well, what do I do if Anson calls
about substantiation procedure?</i>

17
00:04:14,956 --> 00:04:17,790
Have your secretary say
you're in a meeting.

18
00:04:17,825 --> 00:04:19,591
Thank you, Jack.

19
00:04:19,626 --> 00:04:21,366
- Yes, Maria?
- Invitations.

20
00:04:21,694 --> 00:04:23,571
- The Museum Gala.
- No.

21
00:04:23,796 --> 00:04:26,833
- Fitzwilliam Botanical Annual Fundraiser.
- No.

22
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:30,237
- The Hinchberger wedding.
- Hinchberger wedding?

23
00:04:31,104 --> 00:04:34,244
Tuxedos, droning conversations,
I don't think so, no.

24
00:04:34,807 --> 00:04:38,378
I'll send your regrets.
Honestly, why must I bother?

25
00:04:38,413 --> 00:04:40,178
If you don't know about society,

26
00:04:40,213 --> 00:04:43,082
you don't have
the satisfaction of avoiding it.

27
00:04:44,183 --> 00:04:47,027
- Yes?
- I have an Elizabeth on line three.

28
00:04:49,055 --> 00:04:51,831
Your ex-wife.
- I know who it is.

29
00:04:53,059 --> 00:04:54,697
Take a message.

30
00:04:55,194 --> 00:04:56,866
Oh, happy birthday, sir.

31
00:04:58,564 --> 00:05:00,270
Thank you, Maggie.

32
00:05:03,036 --> 00:05:04,569
I don't like her.

33
00:05:04,604 --> 00:05:07,974
I wouldn't mention the following,
but he was very insistent.

34
00:05:08,009 --> 00:05:10,308
- It's a prank.
- What?

35
00:05:10,343 --> 00:05:14,347
A gentleman called requesting lunch.
I assured him you were busy...

36
00:05:14,382 --> 00:05:17,418
- What's his name?
- A Mr. Seymour Butts.

37
00:05:19,652 --> 00:05:22,359
Uhder The Bleachers by Seymour Butts.

38
00:05:23,189 --> 00:05:24,565
I'm sorry?

39
00:05:25,558 --> 00:05:29,529
Cancel my lunch. Make a reservation
at the City Club for myself and Mr. Butts.

40
00:05:29,564 --> 00:05:31,099
My usual table.

41
00:05:33,333 --> 00:05:36,177
Maria, put the reservation under my name.

42
00:05:41,574 --> 00:05:44,510
- Are you ready to order, sir?
- No, I'm still waiting.

43
00:05:44,545 --> 00:05:46,216
This was iced tea.

44
00:05:52,552 --> 00:05:55,828
- Conrad, what a surprise.
- Happy birthday, Nickie.

45
00:05:56,356 --> 00:05:59,735
"Seymour Butts".
I'll never get tired of that one.

46
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:01,959
That's why it's a classic.

47
00:06:01,994 --> 00:06:04,364
Nice restaurant. They gave me a free jacket.

48
00:06:04,399 --> 00:06:06,309
I'm sure they'll want it back.

49
00:06:06,432 --> 00:06:09,218
- I remember being here.
- I took you here once.

50
00:06:09,253 --> 00:06:12,004
No, I used to buy crystal meth
off the maître d'.

51
00:06:12,039 --> 00:06:14,138
- Really?
- In college.

52
00:06:14,173 --> 00:06:16,880
<i>- Which college?
- Touchè.</i>

53
00:06:18,144 --> 00:06:19,350
Miss me?

54
00:06:19,946 --> 00:06:22,914
As much as that's possible. You look good.

55
00:06:22,949 --> 00:06:26,862
- So do you. To think I was worried.
- Worried about me?

56
00:06:27,453 --> 00:06:31,093
How long's it been since Mom's funeral?
Two, three years.

57
00:06:32,325 --> 00:06:35,428
- I thought you quit.
- I did, it didn't take.

58
00:06:35,463 --> 00:06:38,408
- You can't smoke here.
- I'm with you.

59
00:06:38,898 --> 00:06:42,072
It's illegal to smoke in restaurants
in California.

60
00:06:42,468 --> 00:06:44,170
Fuck California.

61
00:06:45,271 --> 00:06:47,751
- How are you?
- Couldn't be better.

62
00:06:49,008 --> 00:06:50,942
- Elizabeth?
- We're divorced.

63
00:06:50,977 --> 00:06:53,683
She remarried a pediatrician,
or a gynecologist,

64
00:06:53,718 --> 00:06:56,390
or a pediatric gynecologist.
Lives in Sausalito.

65
00:06:57,183 --> 00:06:59,890
That's too bad. I liked her.

66
00:07:01,120 --> 00:07:04,590
- And what about you?
- You don't keep track of me anymore?

67
00:07:04,891 --> 00:07:07,200
Not since family week at rehab.

68
00:07:07,794 --> 00:07:11,397
So what brings you to town, Conrad?
Everything all right?

69
00:07:11,432 --> 00:07:13,266
- Yeah.
- Need anything?

70
00:07:13,301 --> 00:07:14,745
- No.
- Really?

71
00:07:15,234 --> 00:07:16,734
No, I don't need anything from you.

72
00:07:16,769 --> 00:07:20,339
I just found myself laying naked
on a beach near Ibiza

73
00:07:20,374 --> 00:07:22,527
and all of a sudden it clicked,

74
00:07:22,562 --> 00:07:24,681
October 12th, Nickie's birthday.

75
00:07:25,378 --> 00:07:27,915
- October 11 th.
- Whatever.

76
00:07:33,419 --> 00:07:35,694
- This is for you.
- You shouldn't have.

77
00:07:36,456 --> 00:07:39,527
What do you get for the man
who has everything?

78
00:07:43,963 --> 00:07:46,085
Consumer Recreation Services.

79
00:07:46,120 --> 00:07:48,208
Well, I do have golf clubs.

80
00:07:48,568 --> 00:07:49,944
Call that number.

81
00:07:50,470 --> 00:07:53,576
- Why?
- It'll make your life fun.

82
00:07:54,674 --> 00:07:56,641
- Fun.
- You know what that is?

83
00:07:56,676 --> 00:08:00,880
You've seen other people have it.
It's an entertainment service.

84
00:08:00,915 --> 00:08:02,654
An escort service?

85
00:08:03,449 --> 00:08:05,618
A profound life experience.

86
00:08:07,353 --> 00:08:10,766
Sorry. I'll just get...
- Nah, it's all right, just...

87
00:08:12,124 --> 00:08:14,060
- Tell me you'll call.
- I said I would.

88
00:08:14,095 --> 00:08:15,368
No, you didn't.

89
00:08:17,697 --> 00:08:19,642
You still on medication?

90
00:08:20,900 --> 00:08:23,719
- Why would you say that?
You gentlemen ready to order?

91
00:08:23,754 --> 00:08:26,539
- I didn't mean how it sounded.
- I'm not on anything.

92
00:08:26,574 --> 00:08:29,842
I'm not even seeing a shrink.
And I feel great.

93
00:08:29,877 --> 00:08:31,252
Do you mind?

94
00:08:35,081 --> 00:08:37,248
I think you'll like this. I did.

95
00:08:37,283 --> 00:08:39,990
It was the best thing
that ever happened to me.

96
00:08:40,520 --> 00:08:43,159
- I'll call them.
- Do it. For you.

97
00:08:43,523 --> 00:08:46,058
I said I'll call, okay? All right, I'll call.

98
00:08:46,093 --> 00:08:48,595
It's just that... I hate surprises.

99
00:08:49,962 --> 00:08:51,264
I know.

100
00:08:53,266 --> 00:09:00,371
<i>Happy birthday to you</i>

101
00:09:00,406 --> 00:09:04,319
<i>Happy birthday, Mr. Van Orton</i>

102
00:09:04,544 --> 00:09:08,253
<i>Happy birthday to you</i>

103
00:09:13,753 --> 00:09:17,063
<i>Someone's gotta
drive a stake through Anson's heart.</i>

104
00:09:17,356 --> 00:09:18,824
<i>Have you seen
the profitability report?</i>

105
00:09:18,859 --> 00:09:21,192
Lmagine how unhappy I am.

106
00:09:21,227 --> 00:09:23,563
<i>You'll deal with Anson?
- Correct.</i>

107
00:09:23,598 --> 00:09:25,098
<i>We're furious.</i>

108
00:09:25,731 --> 00:09:28,108
<i>- Sleep well, Nicholas.
- I will.</i>

109
00:10:28,194 --> 00:10:29,760
Good night, Ilsa.

110
00:10:29,795 --> 00:10:34,141
- Dinner's in the oven.
- Thank you. Oh, I saw Conrad today.

111
00:10:34,867 --> 00:10:37,745
- Did you? How is he?
- He's okay.

112
00:10:38,104 --> 00:10:41,414
I think he's in a personal improvement cult.

113
00:10:41,707 --> 00:10:44,119
Well, send my love if you see him again.

114
00:10:44,677 --> 00:10:46,850
- Happy birthday.
- Good night.

115
00:11:15,941 --> 00:11:17,977
<i>...companies that defaulted
on their payments.</i>

116
00:11:19,078 --> 00:11:24,960
<i>The FCC expects to receive
more than $500 million in bids...</i>

117
00:11:26,018 --> 00:11:29,288
<i>- Elizabeth, good evening.
- Happy birthday, Nickie.</i>

118
00:11:29,323 --> 00:11:32,428
11:40. You almost missed it this year.

119
00:11:33,426 --> 00:11:37,830
<i>- Have you had a nice birthday?
- Does Rose Kennedy have a black dress?</i>

120
00:11:37,865 --> 00:11:41,402
I went not once, but twice,
through the spanking machine.

121
00:11:42,001 --> 00:11:43,878
<i>I can only imagine.</i>

122
00:11:44,370 --> 00:11:47,640
<i>- How are you?
- Connie asked me the same thing today.</i>

123
00:11:47,675 --> 00:11:50,711
<i>Connie? Really? I always liked your brother.</i>

124
00:11:52,178 --> 00:11:53,452
Anyway...

125
00:11:53,846 --> 00:11:56,916
<i>I just thought this might be difficult for you.</i>

126
00:11:56,951 --> 00:11:58,827
Just another birthday.

127
00:11:59,719 --> 00:12:01,664
<i>Because of your father.</i>

128
00:12:02,421 --> 00:12:06,892
That's right, he was 48, wasn't he?
I hadn't thought about it until now.

129
00:12:08,127 --> 00:12:09,628
<i>Why do I call?</i>

130
00:12:11,063 --> 00:12:12,974
I honestly don't know.

131
00:12:13,265 --> 00:12:16,200
Please send Dr. Mel and Rachel my best.

132
00:12:16,235 --> 00:12:19,978
<i>She has a little brother on the way.
We did the ultrasound.</i>

133
00:12:20,740 --> 00:12:22,742
Official nuclear family.

134
00:12:23,542 --> 00:12:25,342
You must be very pleased.

135
00:12:25,377 --> 00:12:27,948
<i>We are. We are very pleased.</i>

136
00:12:28,647 --> 00:12:31,150
Well, it's good to talk with you...

137
00:12:31,717 --> 00:12:33,584
<i>I guess I should let you go.</i>

138
00:12:33,619 --> 00:12:37,890
<i>- Take care of yourself.
- You too, Nicholas. I mean that, I really do.</i>

139
00:12:37,925 --> 00:12:39,664
Be well. Bye.

140
00:12:40,826 --> 00:12:43,360
<i>A proposal to encourage small businesses</i>

141
00:12:43,395 --> 00:12:46,398
<i>to provide their employees
with health insurance</i>

142
00:12:46,433 --> 00:12:49,366
<i>is now being debated
by Washington legislators.</i>

143
00:12:49,401 --> 00:12:54,273
<i>The bill, which attaches small-business
health plans to those of larger corporations</i>

144
00:12:54,308 --> 00:12:58,476
<i>is receiving initial support
from a number of key Republican leaders.</i>

145
00:12:58,511 --> 00:13:03,050
<i>However, Democratic lawmakers
are voicing serious concerns about the bill,</i>

146
00:13:03,382 --> 00:13:06,192
<i>claiming that it would undermine local...</i>

147
00:13:31,710 --> 00:13:35,146
I just wanted to make sure
that we understand each other.

148
00:13:35,181 --> 00:13:39,459
Once you get it down on paper,
then you can count on my full support.

149
00:13:40,252 --> 00:13:42,321
- We understand.
- Thank you.

150
00:13:42,356 --> 00:13:43,959
We'll talk soon.

151
00:14:20,793 --> 00:14:23,262
You shouldn't feel
this reflects negatively on you.

152
00:14:23,297 --> 00:14:25,332
<i>I do feel this reflects...</i>

153
00:14:25,731 --> 00:14:27,598
- I received this...
- Just a moment.

154
00:14:27,633 --> 00:14:31,337
<i>- I took these tests in good faith.
I'm sorry you feel that way.</i>

155
00:14:31,372 --> 00:14:34,907
We hope we haven't caused you
any inconvenience.

156
00:14:34,942 --> 00:14:37,011
Thank you for choosing CRS.

157
00:14:37,309 --> 00:14:40,045
Mr. Feingold,
could you assist this gentleman?

158
00:14:40,080 --> 00:14:41,580
Oh, yeah, sure.

159
00:14:43,048 --> 00:14:45,282
Jim Feingold, VP Engineering,
Data Analysis.

160
00:14:45,317 --> 00:14:49,560
I don't know exactly how this works.
My brother sent me this card as a gift.

161
00:14:49,955 --> 00:14:53,308
All right, excellent. Let's get started.

162
00:14:53,343 --> 00:14:56,662
I'm sorry about all this hullabaloo here.

163
00:14:56,697 --> 00:14:59,108
We're... Well, we're still moving.

164
00:14:59,798 --> 00:15:02,471
I got an office around here somewhere.

165
00:15:04,737 --> 00:15:06,739
Do you mind holding that?

166
00:15:07,907 --> 00:15:09,716
Thank you very much.

167
00:15:12,344 --> 00:15:13,544
Thank you.

168
00:15:13,579 --> 00:15:16,448
- Are you hungry? New Moon Cafè.
- No, thank you.

169
00:15:16,483 --> 00:15:18,120
Best in Chinatown.

170
00:15:23,122 --> 00:15:25,534
A gift from Conrad Van Orton.

171
00:15:27,059 --> 00:15:28,692
- Interesting.
- What is?

172
00:15:28,727 --> 00:15:32,062
Your brother was a client
with our London branch.

173
00:15:32,097 --> 00:15:36,375
We do a sort of informal scoring.
His numbers are outstanding.

174
00:15:36,769 --> 00:15:38,009
Really?

175
00:15:38,470 --> 00:15:41,206
- You sure you're not hungry?
- No.

176
00:15:41,241 --> 00:15:43,908
Now, you need to fill out these forms.

177
00:15:43,943 --> 00:15:46,745
There's an application
and a couple of psych tests.

178
00:15:46,780 --> 00:15:48,690
The MMPI and the TAT.

179
00:15:49,181 --> 00:15:53,285
For the financial questionnaire,
don't answer anything you don't feel like.

180
00:15:53,320 --> 00:15:54,889
We'll run a TRW.

181
00:15:55,487 --> 00:15:58,763
"I sometimes hurt small animals.
True or false?"

182
00:16:01,393 --> 00:16:03,896
"I feel guilty when I masturbate. "

183
00:16:04,997 --> 00:16:09,401
Well, I don't write the questions.
I just review the answers.

184
00:16:13,706 --> 00:16:17,476
- What is this for?
- A sense of your capabilities...

185
00:16:17,511 --> 00:16:20,422
No, what is this for? What are you selling?

186
00:16:21,747 --> 00:16:22,918
Oh...

187
00:16:23,248 --> 00:16:25,182
- It's a game.
- A game?

188
00:16:25,217 --> 00:16:28,129
Specifically tailored for each participant.

189
00:16:28,687 --> 00:16:33,325
Think of it as a great vacation.
Except you don't go to it, it comes to you.

190
00:16:33,360 --> 00:16:35,532
What kind of vacation?

191
00:16:36,395 --> 00:16:39,933
- It's different every time.
- Humor me with specifics.

192
00:16:40,065 --> 00:16:41,509
We provide

193
00:16:44,737 --> 00:16:46,503
whatever's lacking.

194
00:16:46,538 --> 00:16:49,675
- What if nothing is lacking?
- May I make two suggestions?

195
00:16:49,710 --> 00:16:52,810
You think I'll participate without knowing...

196
00:16:52,845 --> 00:16:56,155
First, admit to yourself
that it sounds intriguing.

197
00:16:57,316 --> 00:16:59,484
Second, you don't have to decide today.

198
00:16:59,519 --> 00:17:01,618
Take the silly test, fill out the forms.

199
00:17:01,653 --> 00:17:04,890
One day, your game begins.
You either love it or hate it.

200
00:17:04,925 --> 00:17:06,391
Decide then.

201
00:17:06,892 --> 00:17:12,398
You know, we're like
an experiential Book-of-the-Month Club.

202
00:17:13,332 --> 00:17:16,904
You can drop out at any time
with no further obligation.

203
00:17:18,637 --> 00:17:20,616
That was my sales pitch.

204
00:17:21,373 --> 00:17:25,444
- How long will this take?
- An hour for those, an hour for the physical.

205
00:17:25,479 --> 00:17:27,644
- Physical?
- It's a cursory examination.

206
00:17:27,679 --> 00:17:31,422
Turn your head and cough.
You'll be out of here in no time.

207
00:18:20,432 --> 00:18:21,706
Confused.

208
00:18:27,539 --> 00:18:28,710
Risky.

209
00:18:32,578 --> 00:18:33,818
Bloody.

210
00:18:38,417 --> 00:18:39,657
Whoops.

211
00:18:59,872 --> 00:19:03,842
<i>Mr. Sutherland called.
Make it Wednesday.</i>

212
00:19:03,877 --> 00:19:05,978
<i>Anson Baer called about tomorrow.</i>

213
00:19:06,013 --> 00:19:07,244
Tomorrow?

214
00:19:07,279 --> 00:19:11,083
- How much longer will this take?
- Not long, you're almost done.

215
00:19:11,118 --> 00:19:14,291
You said that two hours ago.
Put it back until tomorrow.

216
00:19:15,320 --> 00:19:16,954
<i>...following emotional responses.</i>

217
00:19:16,989 --> 00:19:20,436
<i>You have 0.9 seconds
to enter your emotional response.</i>

218
00:19:20,759 --> 00:19:22,829
<i>Reaction time is a factor.</i>

219
00:19:25,631 --> 00:19:28,667
<i>Please make a selection
for the following emotional responses.</i>

220
00:19:28,702 --> 00:19:32,137
<i>You have 0.9 seconds
to enter your emotional response.</i>

221
00:19:35,207 --> 00:19:36,981
Does this thing end?

222
00:19:52,691 --> 00:19:54,992
- I'm sorry to keep you waiting.
- Don't worry.

223
00:19:55,027 --> 00:19:58,530
It's been terrific spending my entire day
with your crack team.

224
00:19:58,565 --> 00:20:01,833
It's all down to this,
an insurance company requirement.

225
00:20:01,868 --> 00:20:04,069
It states you're aware that The Game exists,

226
00:20:04,104 --> 00:20:06,236
that you're willing to participate in it.

227
00:20:06,271 --> 00:20:12,211
Payment is at your brother's discretion,
dependent on your satisfaction.

228
00:20:12,246 --> 00:20:14,680
If I'm not happy, he doesn't have to pay?

229
00:20:14,715 --> 00:20:17,115
We've never had an unsatisfied customer.

230
00:20:17,150 --> 00:20:19,516
I think you mean "dissatisfied. "

231
00:20:19,551 --> 00:20:21,486
That's right.
You're a left-brain word fetishist.

232
00:20:21,521 --> 00:20:25,024
Initials. And sign there. In blood.

233
00:20:25,524 --> 00:20:27,003
Just kidding.

234
00:20:28,827 --> 00:20:32,130
All right, your copy will be at the front desk
in this folder.

235
00:20:32,165 --> 00:20:34,803
And keep the pen. We'll let you know.

236
00:20:39,204 --> 00:20:41,738
<i>Monday or Tuesday?
Both bad.</i>

237
00:20:41,773 --> 00:20:45,015
<i>- How about tonight?
- I'm working all this evening.</i>

238
00:20:46,144 --> 00:20:48,947
<i>Wednesday's the only possibility right now.</i>

239
00:20:48,982 --> 00:20:51,715
<i>You wanna do dinner?
- Okay.</i>

240
00:20:51,750 --> 00:20:54,718
<i>By the way, I went to CRS.</i>

241
00:20:54,753 --> 00:20:56,954
<i>Really? What'd you think?</i>

242
00:20:56,989 --> 00:21:00,190
<i>Well, they seemed
just a little bit disorganized.</i>

243
00:21:00,225 --> 00:21:03,213
<i>When I did it in London,
they'd been around a while.</i>

244
00:21:03,248 --> 00:21:06,201
<i>- Are you gonna do this?
- I haven't decided yet.</i>

245
00:21:09,501 --> 00:21:13,438
It's like getting in on the ground floor
of the next Disneyland.

246
00:21:13,473 --> 00:21:16,441
CRS won't go public.
They're family-owned.

247
00:21:17,476 --> 00:21:21,253
Stranger things have happened.
No, they haven't, actually.

248
00:21:22,080 --> 00:21:25,151
They opened here.
The Game in San Francisco?

249
00:21:25,617 --> 00:21:28,461
See? They're doing fine without any of us.

250
00:21:30,322 --> 00:21:32,028
A scotch and soda.

251
00:21:33,925 --> 00:21:36,746
- Nicholas, how are you?
- James, good evening.

252
00:21:36,781 --> 00:21:39,567
- Got new members here?
- I believe so, sir.

253
00:21:40,499 --> 00:21:43,402
- Put their next round on me.
- Gotcha, sir.

254
00:21:46,605 --> 00:21:51,843
No, no, no. Last time I played Pebble,
I swore I'd never pick up a club again.

255
00:21:53,145 --> 00:21:55,245
Well, speaking about games,

256
00:21:55,280 --> 00:21:58,351
I couldn't help but overhear you talking
about CRS.

257
00:22:00,686 --> 00:22:03,858
The reason I mention it
is I took the test today.

258
00:22:03,893 --> 00:22:07,031
- The Montgomery street office.
- You did? Kudos.

259
00:22:07,326 --> 00:22:10,661
- So yours hasn't started yet?
- Not yet.

260
00:22:10,696 --> 00:22:14,609
That was one of the questions I had for you.
What is it?

261
00:22:15,334 --> 00:22:18,036
- What is it?
- The eternal question.

262
00:22:18,071 --> 00:22:19,703
You know, I envy you.

263
00:22:19,738 --> 00:22:24,209
I wish I could go back
and do it for the first time, all over again.

264
00:22:26,678 --> 00:22:29,818
Here's to new experiences.

265
00:22:33,685 --> 00:22:36,621
Excuse me, I've gotta go. Night, Ted.

266
00:22:36,656 --> 00:22:38,998
- Nicholas.
- Nice to meet you.

267
00:22:42,861 --> 00:22:46,274
- So you played recently?
- About a year ago.

268
00:22:46,998 --> 00:22:49,637
I was working in Los Angeles.

269
00:22:49,935 --> 00:22:52,869
I hear the London office is very good, too.

270
00:22:52,904 --> 00:22:55,884
Sounds like a lot
of fantasy role-playing nonsense.

271
00:22:58,610 --> 00:23:00,646
You wanna know what it is?

272
00:23:00,979 --> 00:23:02,753
What it's all about?

273
00:23:03,281 --> 00:23:06,227
John, chapter 9, verse 25.

274
00:23:06,852 --> 00:23:10,355
I haven't been to Sunday school
in a long time.

275
00:23:10,390 --> 00:23:13,893
"Whereas once I was blind, now I can see. "

276
00:23:16,628 --> 00:23:19,802
Good night, Nicholas. Best of luck.

277
00:23:20,565 --> 00:23:22,009
Good night.

278
00:23:36,214 --> 00:23:38,557
If the Baer/Grant meeting

279
00:23:40,185 --> 00:23:42,494
is not held tomorrow,

280
00:23:43,221 --> 00:23:45,667
it may as well not be held at all.

281
00:23:47,959 --> 00:23:51,129
When Mr. Van Orton boards his plane
on the morrow,

282
00:23:51,164 --> 00:23:53,530
he will have every agreement
and addendum,

283
00:23:53,565 --> 00:23:56,767
the complete closing package,
flawlessly revised.

284
00:23:56,802 --> 00:24:00,305
So you'll miss another opera
you would have slept during anyway.

285
00:24:01,373 --> 00:24:02,773
We now have an opportunity
to show our client

286
00:24:02,808 --> 00:24:06,153
how well we will rise
to his exhilarating challenge.

287
00:24:08,280 --> 00:24:10,414
<i>- Yes.
Nicholas Van Orton?</i>

288
00:24:10,449 --> 00:24:13,016
<i>- Who is this?
- This is Cynthia from CRS.</i>

289
00:24:13,051 --> 00:24:16,321
<i>- How did you get this number?
- We've processed your application.</i>

290
00:24:16,356 --> 00:24:19,529
<i>- I'm in a meeting...
- I'm afraid it was rejected.</i>

291
00:24:20,525 --> 00:24:21,625
I beg your pardon?

292
00:24:21,660 --> 00:24:25,095
<i>You shouldn't feel
this reflects negatively on you.</i>

293
00:24:25,130 --> 00:24:28,099
<i>- This is ridiculous.
- Thank you for thinking of CRS.</i>

294
00:24:29,601 --> 00:24:32,604
- Anything wrong?
- No, nothing.

295
00:24:35,440 --> 00:24:38,844
<i>You may hang up,
or press "pound" for more options.</i>

296
00:24:38,879 --> 00:24:41,654
<i>Oh, this is Conrad, leave a message.</i>

297
00:24:42,647 --> 00:24:46,390
Yes, Connie, it's Nicholas.
Give me a ring when you get in.

298
00:24:46,618 --> 00:24:52,357
Oh, regarding your birthday present,
things are a little crazy right now,

299
00:24:52,392 --> 00:24:54,191
I don't know if I can fit it in my schedule.

300
00:24:54,226 --> 00:24:57,571
We'll talk about it at dinner tomorrow.
Thanks. Bye.

301
00:25:30,896 --> 00:25:32,067
Hello?

302
00:26:33,291 --> 00:26:37,762
<i>...resulting rise of unemployment
and decline in viable small businesses.</i>

303
00:26:38,296 --> 00:26:41,299
<i>Meanwhile, Republican leaders argue
that its passage would be</i>

304
00:26:41,334 --> 00:26:44,473
<i>the very stimulant
a sluggish economy needs.</i>

305
00:26:45,036 --> 00:26:48,173
<i>No one has expressed an opinion
as to how it will impact</i>

306
00:26:48,208 --> 00:26:51,207
<i>the pampered existence
of Nicholas Van Orton.</i>

307
00:26:51,242 --> 00:26:54,544
<i>The one thing on which both
Democrats and Republicans agree</i>

308
00:26:54,579 --> 00:26:59,233
<i>is that most Americans harbor
serious doubts about the economic future.</i>

309
00:26:59,268 --> 00:27:03,888
<i>A recent poll suggests a staggering
57% of American workers believe</i>

310
00:27:03,923 --> 00:27:06,791
<i>there is a very real chance
they will be unemployed</i>

311
00:27:06,826 --> 00:27:09,225
<i>within the next five to seven years.</i>

312
00:27:09,260 --> 00:27:13,708
<i>But what does that matter
to a bloated, millionaire fat cat like you?</i>

313
00:27:14,833 --> 00:27:18,935
<i>Stock markets rose
both domestically and abroad</i>

314
00:27:18,970 --> 00:27:21,671
<i>after the announcement
of stronger than expected earnings</i>

315
00:27:21,706 --> 00:27:25,910
<i>by several hi-tech companies,
but dipped again after reports</i>

316
00:27:25,945 --> 00:27:28,515
<i>that Nicholas Van Orton had sneezed.</i>

317
00:27:31,449 --> 00:27:35,920
<i>Are you gonna spend the rest of the evening
prying at that clown's mouth?</i>

318
00:27:37,122 --> 00:27:38,288
I... I don't...

319
00:27:38,323 --> 00:27:41,633
<i>It's frustrating for me
if you don't pay attention.</i>

320
00:27:42,661 --> 00:27:44,162
What is this?

321
00:27:44,929 --> 00:27:48,103
<i>This is your game, Nicholas,
and welcome to it.</i>

322
00:27:49,534 --> 00:27:52,571
<i>I'm here to let you in on a few ground rules.</i>

323
00:27:53,071 --> 00:27:56,313
<i>You've received the very first key
and others will follow.</i>

324
00:27:56,574 --> 00:28:00,178
<i>You won't know where you'll find them
or how you'll need to use them,</i>

325
00:28:00,213 --> 00:28:02,123
<i>so keep your eyes open.</i>

326
00:28:02,514 --> 00:28:06,689
<i>- How do you... You can see me?
- Let's save the questions till after.</i>

327
00:28:09,287 --> 00:28:11,027
How does this work?

328
00:28:12,657 --> 00:28:15,899
<i>There's a tiny camera
looking at you right now.</i>

329
00:28:17,762 --> 00:28:20,765
<i>- That's impossible.
- You're right. Impossible.</i>

330
00:28:21,132 --> 00:28:23,976
<i>You're having a conversation
with your television.</i>

331
00:28:27,072 --> 00:28:28,744
<i>It's miniaturized.</i>

332
00:28:31,576 --> 00:28:34,056
<i>Do you know how dangerous that is?</i>

333
00:28:38,883 --> 00:28:40,083
Mr. Van Orton?

334
00:28:40,118 --> 00:28:41,786
<i>And in domestic news, in southern...</i>

335
00:28:41,821 --> 00:28:43,553
Yes, Ilsa, what is it?

336
00:28:43,588 --> 00:28:46,068
- Is everything all right?
- Fine...

337
00:28:46,558 --> 00:28:49,125
<i>...in a petroleum processing
plant yesterday...</i>

338
00:28:49,160 --> 00:28:52,130
I've finished for the evening.
Will you be needing anything else?

339
00:28:52,165 --> 00:28:53,797
No, thank you.

340
00:28:53,832 --> 00:28:56,366
- Good night.
- Good night, then.

341
00:28:56,401 --> 00:28:59,704
<i>...broke out
in a petroleum processing plant yesterday.</i>

342
00:28:59,739 --> 00:29:01,538
<i>Several workers were injured...</i>

343
00:29:01,573 --> 00:29:04,280
<i>- Who's that?
- Never mind who that is.</i>

344
00:29:04,676 --> 00:29:06,611
<i>You wanna know
how a camera got into your home.</i>

345
00:29:06,646 --> 00:29:07,851
Yes, I would.

346
00:29:16,221 --> 00:29:17,324
<i>Cold.</i>

347
00:29:19,324 --> 00:29:20,530
<i>Colder.</i>

348
00:29:24,763 --> 00:29:25,900
<i>Warm.</i>

349
00:29:30,602 --> 00:29:31,842
<i>Warmer.</i>

350
00:29:47,819 --> 00:29:49,457
<i>Write this number down.</i>

351
00:29:49,687 --> 00:29:53,057
<i>It's a 24-hour
Consumer Recreation Services hotline,</i>

352
00:29:53,092 --> 00:29:54,791
<i>for emergencies only.</i>

353
00:29:54,826 --> 00:29:57,695
<i>But don't call asking
what the object of the game is.</i>

354
00:29:57,730 --> 00:30:00,903
<i>Figuring that out is the object of the game.</i>

355
00:30:01,332 --> 00:30:05,280
<i>Good luck,
and congratulations on choosing CRS.</i>

356
00:30:05,570 --> 00:30:09,108
<i>...threatened to have
the American ambassador expelled.</i>

357
00:30:09,774 --> 00:30:13,244
<i>The US State Department responded quickly
with a formal apology,</i>

358
00:30:13,279 --> 00:30:16,748
<i>and announcement
of the ambassador's immediate removal.</i>

359
00:30:37,235 --> 00:30:39,112
Help me out here, huh?

360
00:30:39,804 --> 00:30:41,613
I used to be in Nam.

361
00:30:54,452 --> 00:30:58,161
<i>If you'd like to
make a call, please hang up and try again.</i>

362
00:32:14,232 --> 00:32:15,767
May I help you?

363
00:32:19,837 --> 00:32:21,213
What is it?

364
00:32:25,410 --> 00:32:26,513
What?

365
00:32:35,753 --> 00:32:37,095
Thank you.

366
00:32:50,501 --> 00:32:51,570
Bud?

367
00:32:54,138 --> 00:32:55,912
Can you help me out?

368
00:32:57,775 --> 00:33:00,346
I need some toilet paper, I ran out.

369
00:33:02,580 --> 00:33:04,719
Just go into the next stall.

370
00:33:07,318 --> 00:33:09,161
Come on, help me out.

371
00:33:10,355 --> 00:33:14,362
Hello?

372
00:33:19,530 --> 00:33:22,967
- Didn't expect to see you here.
- I wanted to wish you luck.

373
00:33:23,002 --> 00:33:26,337
- You been up all night?
- I checked it. Personally.

374
00:33:26,372 --> 00:33:28,771
I appreciate that.
Go home and get some sleep.

375
00:33:28,806 --> 00:33:31,115
- You sure you don't want me along?
- No, I'll be fine.

376
00:33:32,610 --> 00:33:34,851
Nice shirt.
- Don't ask.

377
00:33:35,580 --> 00:33:36,717
<i>Ciao.</i>

378
00:34:01,539 --> 00:34:03,639
All these years,

379
00:34:03,674 --> 00:34:07,111
the first time ever you step foot
in these offices,

380
00:34:07,146 --> 00:34:09,345
it's to ask me to step down.

381
00:34:09,380 --> 00:34:11,482
You promised to meet
the projections, Anson.

382
00:34:11,517 --> 00:34:13,825
$1.60 a share is what you said.

383
00:34:14,252 --> 00:34:17,289
I don't think this visit comes as a surprise.

384
00:34:17,688 --> 00:34:20,456
- The projections were too optimistic.
- Admittedly, yes.

385
00:34:20,491 --> 00:34:23,781
Our EPS was 150 last quarter.
We are up eight cents per share.

386
00:34:23,816 --> 00:34:27,072
The expectations were ten.
The expectations meant everything.

387
00:34:28,032 --> 00:34:30,569
Will you hold me to it over pennies?

388
00:34:30,935 --> 00:34:33,037
My stock is falling,
I don't know about yours.

389
00:34:33,072 --> 00:34:35,271
Those pennies are costing millions.

390
00:34:35,306 --> 00:34:39,911
Give me next quarter.
If you still feel this way, vote your shares.

391
00:34:39,946 --> 00:34:42,084
Today is what counts, Anson.

392
00:34:45,116 --> 00:34:48,620
You... You intractable son of a bitch.

393
00:34:49,587 --> 00:34:52,073
If your father could see you now.

394
00:34:52,108 --> 00:34:54,524
- What?
- Your father was a friend.

395
00:34:54,559 --> 00:34:56,859
A friend.
God damn it, I watched you grow up.

396
00:34:56,894 --> 00:35:02,600
Because you went fishing with my father,
I should watch you throw my money away?

397
00:35:02,635 --> 00:35:04,079
- I was...
- I'm not finished.

398
00:35:04,635 --> 00:35:08,173
You misspoke before
when you said you're stepping down.

399
00:35:09,040 --> 00:35:10,678
I am firing you.

400
00:35:11,609 --> 00:35:14,453
Action is taken, confidence is restored.

401
00:35:15,446 --> 00:35:17,413
- The stock goes up.
- No.

402
00:35:17,448 --> 00:35:20,155
There's no Baer/Grant Publishing...

403
00:35:20,785 --> 00:35:25,097
No Baer/Grant Publishing
without Anson Baer.

404
00:35:25,923 --> 00:35:27,156
Where's Stewart Grant?

405
00:35:27,191 --> 00:35:30,194
Probably out sailing,
enjoying his golden years,

406
00:35:30,229 --> 00:35:32,970
wondering where the hell you are, Anson.

407
00:35:33,164 --> 00:35:34,506
I'm sorry.

408
00:35:35,500 --> 00:35:36,910
You failed.

409
00:35:40,972 --> 00:35:45,978
I have prepared a severance package
that is more than equitable.

410
00:35:47,545 --> 00:35:50,116
Valid until this evening.

411
00:35:52,450 --> 00:35:54,554
I could fight you on this.

412
00:35:55,052 --> 00:35:59,694
If I leave without your signature,
this agreement begins to disintegrate.

413
00:36:00,758 --> 00:36:06,298
Benefits shrink, options narrow,
and your compensation will be shriveled.

414
00:36:12,770 --> 00:36:16,479
I think it's in your best interest, Anson, to...

415
00:36:32,623 --> 00:36:35,899
Well, I guess this is your lucky day.

416
00:36:37,962 --> 00:36:40,601
My lawyers will be in touch with you.

417
00:37:13,331 --> 00:37:16,432
Excuse me,
has Conrad Van Orton left a message?

418
00:37:16,467 --> 00:37:19,709
- I'll check right away, Mr. Van Orton.
- Thank you.

419
00:37:26,110 --> 00:37:28,249
I'm so sorry. Here.

420
00:37:28,646 --> 00:37:32,750
- Please don't do that.
- I'm so sorry. I wasn't even looking.

421
00:37:32,785 --> 00:37:35,620
- I'm really having a bad day.
- How about a bad month?

422
00:37:35,655 --> 00:37:38,691
You did almost the same thing
to me last week.

423
00:37:39,023 --> 00:37:40,289
- Here.
- Don't do that.

424
00:37:40,324 --> 00:37:43,703
Just get me some napkins
and some soda water.

425
00:37:44,028 --> 00:37:47,907
It looks like the cleaning bill's
gonna be more than the suit.

426
00:37:49,033 --> 00:37:50,671
I don't think so.

427
00:37:52,036 --> 00:37:54,405
- It was an accident, sir.
- Terrific.

428
00:37:54,440 --> 00:37:56,145
I said I was sorry.

429
00:37:59,877 --> 00:38:01,978
- Asshole.
- Christine.

430
00:38:02,013 --> 00:38:06,100
- Mr. Van Orton is a valued customer.
- Fine. Then you kiss his ass.

431
00:38:06,135 --> 00:38:10,187
- Don't you talk to me like that.
- I apologized, I offered to help.

432
00:38:10,222 --> 00:38:12,156
- Clean out your locker.
- What?

433
00:38:12,191 --> 00:38:13,657
You heard me.

434
00:38:16,761 --> 00:38:18,961
I'm terribly sorry, Mr. Van Orton.

435
00:38:18,996 --> 00:38:22,238
Will this table suit you
for a complimentary meal?

436
00:38:22,500 --> 00:38:24,035
It'll be fine.

437
00:38:32,143 --> 00:38:34,680
- I'll fetch your waiter.
- Thank you.

438
00:38:35,546 --> 00:38:36,922
Check, sir.

439
00:38:59,704 --> 00:39:01,906
Fuck you and your vichyssoise, you little...

440
00:39:01,941 --> 00:39:04,208
Miss? Pardon me, miss.

441
00:39:04,243 --> 00:39:05,908
Great, it's you.

442
00:39:05,943 --> 00:39:10,789
I don't know how this works,
but do you have something for me? I...

443
00:39:12,683 --> 00:39:16,387
- I received this note.
- What are you babbling about, psycho?

444
00:39:16,422 --> 00:39:18,487
I need to know what is going on.

445
00:39:18,522 --> 00:39:21,090
I'm going on my second job this month,

446
00:39:21,125 --> 00:39:23,730
and now it looks
like I'm going on unemployment.

447
00:39:24,462 --> 00:39:28,466
- Maybe I should try to explain...
- Don't explain, just fuck off.

448
00:39:31,369 --> 00:39:33,280
Son of a bitch.

449
00:39:33,838 --> 00:39:37,012
I'm trying to... I am apologizing.

450
00:39:50,154 --> 00:39:51,928
Jesus. What is this?

451
00:39:55,960 --> 00:39:58,060
- What's with him?
- He just fell down.

452
00:39:58,095 --> 00:40:00,664
Sir, can you hear me?
Do you know what to do?

453
00:40:00,699 --> 00:40:02,336
I don't even know if he's breathing.

454
00:40:04,235 --> 00:40:07,682
Move your head. Don't stand there, get help.

455
00:40:09,006 --> 00:40:10,815
Hey, hey. It's okay.

456
00:40:11,809 --> 00:40:14,755
- We're gonna get help.
- How do we know he's real?

457
00:40:15,246 --> 00:40:18,317
There's piss in his pants,
is that real enough for you?

458
00:40:19,316 --> 00:40:22,092
- Holy God.
- He's turning blue.

459
00:40:23,487 --> 00:40:24,897
Police.

460
00:40:36,434 --> 00:40:39,437
- You have to fill these forms out.
- I don't know this man.

461
00:40:39,472 --> 00:40:41,270
I can't get involved.

462
00:40:41,305 --> 00:40:43,672
- I have to detain you.
- Detain me?

463
00:40:43,707 --> 00:40:46,159
- The report's gotta be filled out.
- She can do it.

464
00:40:46,194 --> 00:40:48,612
Ride with your wife,
we'll meet at the hospital.

465
00:40:48,647 --> 00:40:50,350
Two blocks away.

466
00:40:50,385 --> 00:40:52,053
She's not my wife.

467
00:40:58,189 --> 00:41:02,426
This is insane. Ten minutes ago,
I was enjoying a quiet little dinner.

468
00:41:02,461 --> 00:41:04,337
I received this note.

469
00:41:08,332 --> 00:41:10,772
- He's breathing, isn't he?
- Yes.

470
00:41:10,807 --> 00:41:13,213
Is the siren entirely necessary?

471
00:41:14,672 --> 00:41:15,878
Right.

472
